Killing most hostile monsters gives a point or more,
and many monsters do not leave corpses.  So long as
you leave behind some number of peacefulls early on
you'll build enough alignment points early in the
game that later even that won't matter.

Let's see if I get this right - Monks exercise their
wisdom by going around Weak.  Any character abuses
strength by going around weak.  Exercising a stat
can increase its max, abusing a stat can reduce its
current without reducing its max.  Thus for a Monk
going around weak is mixed with a net benefit once
you know how to handle the problems.  That plus
once a Monk's wisdom gets high enough spells no longer
cost nutrition (like when a wizard's intelligence
gets high enough).

Where most early characters are ready to pray to
cure their hunger as soon as they hit Weak, a monk
benfits by counting turns after the Weak status
appears to the first faint and should then wait a
set number of turns after turning Weak in
subsequent rounds.  Until maxing out wisdom, after
having a way to handle declining strength, baring
other needs for prayer and all those usual Nethack
caveats.



